Exploratory Data Analysis about Lung Cancer Mortality in the U.S. from 2000 to 2019


Overview:::
This project analyzes lung cancer mortality trends in the United States from 2000 to 2019 using data from the IHME Global Burden of Disease Study.
We explore national patterns, demographic disparities, geographic differences, and how these have evolved over two decades.

Dataset:::
Source: IHME – U.S. Lung Cancer Mortality from 2000 to 2019

Observations: about 9 million after cleaning

Key Variables: year, state, sex, age_group, race, mortality_rate


Research Questions:::
How have national mortality rates changed over time?

How do rates differ by sex, age, race, and state?

Have disparities narrowed or persisted?


Methods:::
Data cleaning using tidyverse and janitor.

Visualization of trends with ggplot2 and animated U.S. maps via gganimate.

Statistical modeling with linear regression to assess predictors of mortality.


Key Findings:::
National Decline: Mortality fell around 25% which decline from 112 to 82 per 100000 people from 2000 to 2019.

Sex Disparities: Males had higher rates than females. Both  gender declined, but female decline slower.

Age Patterns: Highest mortality in 65 and higher age. Under 50 remained low and stable.

State Differences: Highest in KY, WV, MS and lowest in UT, CO.

Racial Disparities:

  Highest: American Indian/Alaska Native

  Lowest: Asian

  Black mortality dropped sharply, nearing parity with Whites by 2019.

Regression: Yearly mortality drops about 2.19 per 100000 people, also demographic effects stable over time.

